{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to The Economist for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2720,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2014, the pale electromagnetic ghosts of an \nancient exploded star barely grazed the camera  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10240,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Hubble Space Telescope. Four points of dim \nlight revealing the same supernova, framing the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17440,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy whose gravitational field had bent this \nlight to us. After a 9.3 billion year journey,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the images arrived in rapid succession. \nBut other, longer paths wove the spacetime  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30320,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contortions of the surrounding galaxy cluster. \nAstronomers spun up their models and predicted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a single explosive encore the following \nyear. They waited, they prepared Hubble,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43440,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and 12 months later their it was -- the laggard \nafterimage of the supernova just as predicted.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51040,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ridiculous that we were even able to predict \nthis. But more incredible is its scientific  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58160,
    "dDurationMs": 9360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "value. In a 1964 paper, Sjur Refsdal pointed out \nthat the time-delay between gravitationally lensed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67520,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernova images could give a way to measure \nthe expansion rate of the whole universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73920,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've now done that with the eponymous Supernova \nRefsdal, but we've now also built this up into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82000,
    "dDurationMs": 11600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what may become the most powerful method \nfor unlocking the nature of dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've got a couple of quick announcements \nbefore we start. First, our eternal battle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "against the algorithm continues. The best way to \nencourage YouTube to share our videos is to like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and comment. Doing both really makes a difference. \nAnd if you're new here, subscribe, hit the bell,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and introduce yourself in the comments. We're \nfriendly. Next up, we're excited to launch our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "composition of the universe nickel and enamel pin \nnow with a holofoil backing. If you want to flex  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your knowledge of dark energy, dark matter, and \nthe terrifyingly small slice of ordinary matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129360,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that you and our world is made of, check out our \nfirst pin of 2026. And if you prefer your mornings  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fueled by dark energy, we've also got a brand new \ndark energy mug. Plus, our UV glow cur rotating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole hoodies and shirts are still available. \nLinks in the description. Now, onto the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've known that the universe is \nexpanding since 1929, and that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion is accelerating since 1998. The \nculprit behind the acceleration is unknown,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159600,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we live with a stand-in term \"dark energy\". Our \nmodern cosmological model assumes that dark energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166640,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has a constant density--always the same amount \nof the outward-shoving stuff per volume of space.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there's recent evidence to the contrary--which \nmay be why our primary efforts to measure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the expansion rate of the universe disagree \nwith each other. Let's review those methods."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the one hand, studying the oldest light in \nthe universe—the cosmic microwave background,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the CMB—gives us exquisite information \nabout a time soon after the Big Bang.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Combined with our cosmological \nmodel we can predict how fast  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199520,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe should be expanding \nnow, and that modern expansion is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "characterized as a measure of the \nHubble constant. On the other hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observations of supernova explosions allow \nus to track that expansion history in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relatively modern universe. Exploding white \ndwarf stars—type 1a supernovae—have very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predictable intrinsic brightnesses, allowing \nus to determine distances based on how faint  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they appear to us. And those distances give \nus another measure of the Hubble constant,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a sense more directly measuring \nthat modern expansion rate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If our measurements are good and our \ncosmological model is good then the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240000,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CMB and supernova results should match. But \nthey don’t. The supernova program finds an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion rate a few percent higher than the \nextrapolation of the CMB measurements. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252720,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "either the CMB or the supernova result is flawed, \nor the cosmological model we use to extrapolate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259680,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between the two is wrong. This so-called hubble \ntension has been lurking around for some years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and stubbornly refuses to go away even \nas we improve our measurements. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hints that we may really need new physics \nin our cosmological model. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that model assumes constant dark energy, but \nwhat if dark energy really changes over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before we jump to conclusions, it’s \nabsolutely critical that we make independent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurements of the expansion history. \nThe supernova results are particularly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293120,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vulnerable to systematic errors because \nthey need to be bootstrapped off a series  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of distance calibration steps. More room \nfor weak links in the calculation chain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304240,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, some would say, me included, \nthat one of the most promising  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "independent methods is something \ncalled time-delay cosmography."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312800,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the method outlined by Sjur Refsdal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315760,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and was applied to the eponymous supernova. But \nit turns out we can also use a different cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324480,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cataclysm that’s far brighter and more \ncommon that the supernova—the quasar—and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331760,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the best time-delay cosmography has been done with \nquasars. So I will start by talking about quasars,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but supernovae do have a bright \nfuture in this field as we’ll see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So a quasar is what you get when the supermassive \nblack hole in the center of every galaxy enters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350320,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a feeding frenzy, as happens from time to \ntime. Active galactic nucleus is more general,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358400,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quasars are the bright ones that we care \nabout. So, matter driven into the galactic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "core is caught in the colossal gravitational \nfield and spirals in to feed the black hole.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370640,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The gas also generates such prodigious heat on \nits way that these “accretion disks” glow bright  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376720,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to be seen across the universe. Quasars \nare messy eaters. The infalling material comes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in fits and bursts and the bright central \nregion fluctuates wildly. This causes the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389440,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire beast to flicker on timescales of hours \nto months. By itself a quasar isn’t great for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398000,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measuring distances. But if we introduce a \nnew level of chaos then the magic happens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404880,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Einstein’s universe, light does not travel \nto us in straight lines. Mass curves spacetime,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and light is bound to follow those curves. \nWe see the effects of gravitational lensing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everywhere in the universe. Most of the \nlensing is subtle—manifesting as changing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424160,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brightness and warping of shapes. But in rare \ncases, a “gravitational lens” is so perfectly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431520,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "situated between us and a distant source that the \nlatter’s light follows multiple paths to reach us.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438800,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We literally see the same object \nthrough different paths through space,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that looks like duplications of the \nsource around the lens. This is “strong  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lensing” and strongly lensed quasars are going \nto become our new cosmic expansion tracker."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455280,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The core concept is simple enough. The 2 or 4 \ndifferent paths taken by the quasar’s light are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different lengths, and so the light we see \nin each of the images has taken a different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of time to reach us. So, although \nwe’re looking at the same quasar in each,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re looking at the quasar as it was at slightly \ndifferent times. And this is where the quasar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "variability comes in. Its brightness fluctuations \nare pretty random, but they still form distinct  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterns in the lightcurve—the plot of brightness \nover time. If we can record the lightcurves of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486880,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different images in one lensed quasar, then we \ncan find where the lightcurve patterns match up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that gives us the time difference of the \npaths of different images. We call this the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499680,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time delay. That in turn gives us a way to measure \nthe actual distances to the lens and the quasar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the first level description. Let’s dig \ndeeper because it’s not as easy as it sounds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up—the actual math. It’s based on \nFermats principle of least time—light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will always take the path between \ntwo points that takes the least time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This can be used to derive the \nbending of light by regular lenses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it also applies to gravitational lenses. The \npaths taken in a gravitational lens represent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "local minima in the light travel time—paths \nfor which, the photon, if deviated slightly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540640,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would take longer to reach us. The path lengths \ngive us one aspect of the time delay. The other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the Shapiro or gravitational time delay— just \npassing through different parts of the galaxy’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555120,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field leads to an additional time \ndifference. The resulting time delay formula is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surprisingly simple for such a complex system. \nAnd the most important thing about this expression  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569120,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that the front term scales with the Hubble \nconstant—with the expansion speed of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the time delay goes straight \nto a Hubble constant measurement. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "none of this depends on a chain \nof other distance measurements."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sounds too easy—so why hasn’t this \nbecome the standard for measuring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591760,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic expansion over the supernovae method?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595520,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well several reasons actually. One is the \nrarity of good gravitationally lensed quasars.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These objects are rare already due to the \nprecise alignment needed between observer,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lens and quasar. And most lensed quasars aren’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "great for this purpose due to the next \nissue: modeling the lensing galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621040,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is by far the biggest challenge. \nGalaxies make pretty terrible lenses compared  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625360,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to precision-ground glass lenses.They are lumpy, \nirregular objects made of stars and dark matter.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632880,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to properly calculate time delays we need \nto know the gravitational field of that galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s kind of amazing that lens modeling is \neven possible at all. One particular trick  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is especially important: we can pretend that \nall of the galaxy’s mass is collapsed into a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652400,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flat plane due to the extreme distances compared \nto the size of the galaxy. This is the thin-lens  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660000,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "approximation, and it turns gravitational lensing \ninto a classic geometric optics problem. So we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "build a lens model based on the light of the \nlensing galaxy’s stars and the positions of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the lensed images themselves. That works pretty \nwell when the lensing galaxy is simple—and that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679760,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requirement for an easily modelable lens reduces \nthe number of useful objects dramatically."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even in the case of a simple lens, there are \nconfounding factors that we can’t completely rule  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out. Even if we can see the light from \nthe lens’s stars, we can’t see its dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter so have to make some assumptions \nabout how it’s spread through the galaxy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704720,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also have to try to model the gravitational \neffects of nearby objects like other galaxies.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the trickiest issues is called the \nmass-sheet degeneracy. We tend to assume  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718080,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the lensing galaxy is the only significant \nsource of bending for a given quasar’s light. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that light is actually traveling billions of \nlight years, and may well pass through other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational fields—other “mass sheets”--on \nthe way. It’s very difficult to rule that out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These issues are best solved by making a \ndifferent type of measurement of the lens’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741840,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field. The stars of a galaxy move \nin the mutual gravity of each other. And it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749760,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible to get a bead on that motion by looking \nfor average Doppler shifts in their starlight.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This lets us build a much less ambiguous \nmap of the galaxy’s gravitational field.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762160,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these sorts of “kinematic” \nmeasurements are very difficult to make,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and typically only possible for \nrelatively nearby lensing galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770560,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, so let’s say we have a perfect \nlens model. How do we get time delays?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well by monitoring the system for years with a \ndecent-sized professional telescope and building  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lightcurve for each image in the lens. \nBut even building the lightcurve isn’t easy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The separation between lens images is tiny and \nthey’re often blended together when observed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795360,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the ground. They need to be pried apart \nwith clever algorithms. That plus the faintness  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these objects means every measurement has \nan uncertainty attached to it. On top of that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can’t monitor continuously. There are gaps in \nthe lightcurve during the day and a long “season  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gap” every year when the Sun gets in the way of \nour lens. All of this adds to the uncertainty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the time delay measurement when we try to line \nup the light curves from different quasar images."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828800,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Combine the observing constraints with the lens \nmodel uncertainties and we end up with some big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836080,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "error bars in our measurement of the time delay,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and in the resulting Hubble \nconstant for any one lens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844720,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so where are we with this whole program? \nIn 2019, the Holicow collaboration published  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their latest measurement of H0 \nbased on the 6 best lensed quasars,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857840,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "getting a value of 73.3 plus or minus. \nAnd just last year Holicow’s successor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865280,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "TDCOSMO improved the analysis and added two \nmore lenses to get a slightly smaller 71.8."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873040,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These numbers are more consistent with the late \nuniverse supernova value than the CMB value,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the error bars are larger that the \nsupernova uncertainties. Taken on their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own the time delay cosmography results \ndon’t confirm the tension between the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891040,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "late and early expansion measurements. \nBut when these new results are actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "combined with the supernova results \nthey actually do strengthen that tension,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and suggest a less than one \nin 10 million chance that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these values are so different due to random noise."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909280,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we’re not quite there in confirming the Hubble \ntension. There could be some unknown systematic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "error that is pushing the supernova \nresult to be higher than the CMB result.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that case the lensing value on its \nown isn’t tight enough to confirm the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tension. Now I’ll come back to \nhow that’s soon going to change."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932960,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First though, quasars are awesome \nfor this work because they are,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps horrifically, extremely abundant \nin the universe. But time-delay cosmography  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was first conceived for use with \nsupernovae, so how supernovae do?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947280,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, precise measurement of time delay for the \nlate 5th image of supernova Refsdal gave us a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hubble constant measurement of around \n70, but with pretty large error bars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961120,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s also the lensed supernova SN H0pe, \nwhich is extra special because it’s the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968800,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sort of type-1a supernova that are used to \ndo our other late universe Hubble constant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurements. So in a sense, this one gave \nus two independent measures of the Hubble  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant in one object. In practice these were \ncombined—the lensing side gave us a fairly "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direct H0 measure, while the \ntype 1a supernova analysis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allowed us to break some of the degeneracies \nin the lensing analysis. H0pe yielded a Hubble  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant that's also consistent with other \nmodern-universe expansion measurements,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006080,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but again with huge error bars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For both of these supernovae, the error \nbars are large, but no larger than we get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013680,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for any single quasar. The advantage of the \nquasars is we have more of them. Statistics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lets us whittle down uncertainty. In general, \nincreasing the number of both lensed quasars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027760,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and lensed supernovae is going to be the key \nto moving forward. And that is about to happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035120,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In early 2026 the Vera Rubin \nObservatory will start its LSST survey,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042320,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which it’ll image the entire \nsouthern sky every few days for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a full 10 years. Combined with other new \nfacilities like ESO’s Euclid satellite,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052400,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we expect to discover thousands new lensed \nquasars and hundreds of lensed supernovae."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1059440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s been estimated that the H0 uncertainty \nwill be pushed down to a percent or less,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which, if it happens, will render \nthe hubble tension incontrovertible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Rubin may be able to do a lot more. \nWith enough lensed quasars and supernovae,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we may be able to do more than measure \nthe modern expansion rate. We may be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1080800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to trace the evolution of the \nexpansion history over cosmic time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086480,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the current standard cosmological \nmodel—Lambda CDM—dark energy is assumed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to have a constant density. But there’s evidence \nthat this isn’t  the case I mentioned earlier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2025 the Dark Energy Spectroscopic \nInstrument team reported the results of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its expansion history study using \nbaryon acoustic oscillations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and obviously we covered that. When \ncombined with the CMB and supernova  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112400,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "results DESI reports strong indication that \ndark energy has weakened over cosmic time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1119040,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was the dark energy news of the decade. If \nwe can measure that change somewhat accurately,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1127040,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can actually start to nail down the physics \nor dark energy—and figure out what the stuff"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually is. Time-delay cosmography \nwith gravitational lensing may be the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139440,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "key to doing that. In fact, this could be the most \nimportant result that will come out of Rubin-LSST,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1147760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and many people are working on it. No big deal \nreally. We just clock the flickering echoes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153360,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a thousand ancient cataclysms and discover \nthe culprit behind our accelerating spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1160400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to The Economist for supporting PBS. \nFrom reporting on scientific innovation to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1165600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the shifting landscape of global politics, The \nEconomist's mission is to deliver comprehensive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1171360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coverage that goes beyond the headlines. The \nEconomist aims to make complex topics accessible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1177280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and engaging, such as a recent long feature \nexploring how we might export life to Mars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1183680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the emerging field of applied astrobiology. \nThe Economist app includes daily journalism,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1190160,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plus subscriber-only podcasts and newsletters, \nnarrated audio articles, and Insider, a new weekly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1198160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "video series where senior editors unpack the \nstories shaping today's world from geopolitics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1203840,
    "dDurationMs": 10320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to technology. As a viewer of PBS Spacetime, you \ncan get a special 35% off to enjoy The Economist."
    } ]
  } ]
}
